---
description: Explain planned changes and get confirmation before writing, editing, or running non-read-only commands
alwaysApply: true
---

# Confirm before acting

Before making any file changes or running any command that is not purely
read-only, explain what you are about to do and why, then wait for the
user's explicit confirmation before proceeding.

- Read-only actions (reading files, searching, listing, running tests or
  linters that don't modify anything, `git status`/`git diff`/`git log`)
  do not require confirmation.
- Anything that writes/edits/deletes a file, or runs a shell command with
  side effects (installs, `git commit`/`push`, deployments, `rm`/`mv`,
  config changes, etc.) requires: a short explanation of what will change
  and why, then explicit user confirmation, before executing.
- Never modify sensitive files (`.env`, credentials, secrets, tokens, SSH
  keys, cloud/CLI auth config) without calling it out explicitly and
  getting confirmation first, even if the broader task was already
  approved.
- Batch related planned actions into one explanation/confirmation rather
  than asking repeatedly for each trivial sub-step.
